concepts.body:
An **async function** always returns a promise. Inside it, **`await`** pauses the function until a promise settles, then gives you the fulfilled value — or throws the rejection reason:

```javascript
async function showOrders(id) {
  try {
    const user = await loadUser(id);
    const orders = await loadOrders(user.id);
    render(orders);
  } catch (err) {
    showError(err);
  }
}
```

It's still promises underneath: the `return` value becomes the promise's value and a `throw` rejects it. Pausing doesn't block the thread; other code runs while the function waits. `await` also works at the top level of ES modules.

The most important performance habit: **don't serialise independent work**. Each `await` waits before the next line starts, so

```javascript
const a = await getA();
const b = await getB();
```

takes the sum of both durations. If they don't depend on each other, start both and await together: `const [a, b] = await Promise.all([getA(), getB()])`.

Loops need care:

- `for...of` with `await` inside processes items **one after another** — correct when order or rate limits matter.
- `array.forEach(async ...)` does **not** wait; `forEach` ignores the returned promises, so the code after it runs immediately and errors go unhandled.
- For concurrent processing, `await Promise.all(items.map(async item => ...))`.

Calling an async function without `await` (or `.catch`) "fires and forgets" it; any error becomes an unhandled rejection. Linters can flag floating promises. Inside `try`, write `return await p` if you want that `catch` to handle `p`'s rejection.

concepts.code:
const sleep = (ms, v) => new Promise(r => setTimeout(() => r(v), ms));

async function getUser(id) { await sleep(50); return { id, name: "Ada" }; }
async function getPosts(id) { await sleep(50); return ["hello", "world"]; }
async function getFails() { await sleep(10); throw new Error("not found"); }

async function main() {
  let t = Date.now();
  const user = await getUser(1);
  const posts = await getPosts(1);                 // waits for getUser first
  console.log("sequential ~100ms:", Date.now() - t >= 100);

  t = Date.now();
  const [u2, p2] = await Promise.all([getUser(1), getPosts(1)]);
  console.log("parallel ~50ms:", Date.now() - t < 100); // true

  try {
    await getFails();
  } catch (err) {
    console.log("caught:", err.message);           // caught: not found
  }
  return `${user.name} has ${posts.length} posts`;
