concepts.body:
`fetch(url, options)` makes an HTTP request and returns a promise for a **Response**. It's built into browsers and into Node.js 18+.

```javascript
const res = await fetch("https://api.example.com/users/1");
if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);
const user = await res.json();
```

The crucial detail: fetch only **rejects on network failure** (offline, DNS error, CORS refusal, abort). An HTTP error like 404 or 500 still *fulfils* — you must check `res.ok` (status 200–299) or `res.status` yourself.

Reading the body is a second async step: `res.json()`, `res.text()`, `res.blob()` or `res.arrayBuffer()`. A body can only be read once.

To send data, set the method, headers and body:

```javascript
await fetch("/api/todos", {
  method: "POST",
  headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
  body: JSON.stringify({ title: "Write docs" }),
});
```

For form uploads, pass a `FormData` as the body and let fetch set the content type. Query strings are easiest to build with `new URL(base)` and `url.searchParams.set(...)`, which handles encoding.

**Cancellation** uses an `AbortSignal`: pass `{ signal: controller.signal }` and call `controller.abort()`, or use `AbortSignal.timeout(5000)` for a timeout. The promise then rejects with an `AbortError` or `TimeoutError`.

In browsers, requests to another origin are subject to **CORS**: the server must allow your origin, otherwise the browser blocks the response and fetch rejects with a generic `TypeError`. Cookies are sent for same-origin requests by default; cross-origin needs `credentials: "include"`.

